(7265772, 1129870, 60173, '<td valign="top">I really wonder how many people knocked this for the "tilted horizon." <br/> <br/>I thought that at first, but then figured it was a sidewalk in the foreground. Well designed sidewalks aren''t level in areas where there''s actually weather. They are indeed tilted to allow for the water to run off rather than remain on the surface and cause damage. I would imagine the same theory applies to ledges as you say this is. Where I live, we get a lot of rain in the winter. There''s enough tilt on some of our sidewalks that my ankles hurt after a time!<br/> <br/>I suppose there''s a lesson we should take away from this. Watch how much of a tilted surface is in a photo where the horizon is a essential piece? I dunno, heh.</td>', 'I really wonder how many people knocked this for the "tilted horizon."  I thought that at first, but then figured it was a sidewalk in the foreground. Well designed sidewalks aren''t level in areas where there''s actually weather. They are indeed tilted to allow for the water to run off rather than remain on the surface and cause damage. I would imagine the same theory applies to ledges as you say this is. Where I live, we get a lot of rain in the winter. There''s enough tilt on some of our sidewalks that my ankles hurt after a time! I suppose there''s a lesson we should take away from this. Watch how much of a tilted surface is in a photo where the horizon is a essential piece? I dunno, heh.', '2014-09-24 11:44:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(7265773, 1129902, 60173, '<td valign="top">The flip-flops make a lot more sense now that I know who the photographer is!</td>', 'The flip-flops make a lot more sense now that I know who the photographer is!', '2014-09-24 11:44:46.000000', NULL, 0),
